one guy used to be a senior banker in  Zimbabwe but when the coup happened  three years ago her connections put her  life at risk she was forced to flee the  country with her children and claimed  asylum in England she now lives in a  small flat isn't allowed to work and is  provided with the equivalents of 43  dollars a week to feed and clothe  herself and her kids that money is not  enough to buy clothes it's not enough -  it can be math maybe to buy food but  it's also difficult for you to meet  other needs like buying clothes it's  difficult for for for for us to top up  it's difficult for us - to pay for  Internet you need to choose when guys  experiences are the norm for asylum  seekers in Britain recently we met up  with Binney an Eritrean who delivers  food to refugees in the northern town of  Middlesbrough he told us he'd found a  woman who hadn't eaten for 17 days and  was on the point of starving to death  when you are over the age of 70 you have  been confined into a 4x4 metres world  sharing a house with another three  people from different cultures and  nationalities on top of that you have  got arthritis health conditions diabetes  and also some things you need someone to  talk to you because that have a great  impact in turn mental health opposition  politicians have been pressing the  government to increase the weekly amount  of money asylum seekers get during the  lockdown and last week there was a rise  of the equivalent of about $3 a week the  government justified it on the ground  that it's above the inflation rate  though in reality it barely makes a  difference many people now happen to  wait for several months if not years for  a decision on their asylum application  and so you'd be living on what is now 39  pounds 60 a week for a long time and the  impact of that over time and just how  hard it can be for people not to be able  to save up to afford to buy clothes for  the the winter or to replace basic  cooking utensils really adds up over  time and we don't think that something  which the government has ever really  taken into consideration asylum seekers  aren't alone the government recently did  a u-turn over the provision of food to  children from the poorest families in  the country  whose parents have told us they had to  choose routinely between feeding their  children or feeding themselves since all  this began the Johnson administration  has made a great deal out of being able  to magic up hundreds of billions to give  to workers who cannot do their jobs  effectively paying them to stay at home  the total costs of giving asylum seekers  some money for a few basic necessities  is by comparison very small indeed but  clearly these things are not about  morality but about political will  Lawrence Lee Al Jazira Downing Street in  London
 

